WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.849 --> 00:00:05.450
The third chapter of The Mandalorian offered
the most explosive, action-filled scenes seen

00:00:05.450 --> 00:00:09.960
on the series yet, and with this newest episode,
it feels as though we're truly off and running

00:00:09.960 --> 00:00:11.020
at last.

00:00:11.020 --> 00:00:15.500
After watching Mando clearly feel conflicted
by dropping off Baby Yoda in exchange for

00:00:15.500 --> 00:00:20.370
all that Beskar steel, he does exactly what
we were all waiting for: busts in to steal

00:00:20.370 --> 00:00:22.800
the baby back and kill his way out again.

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:26.580
After, of course, he gets sick new armor from
his blacksmith.

00:00:26.580 --> 00:00:31.430
Chapter Three ended on a massive one-against-dozens
gunfight in the spaceport square.

00:00:31.430 --> 00:00:35.871
When Mando is at his lowest, with no apparent
means of escape, the rest of the Mandalorian

00:00:35.871 --> 00:00:40.260
enclave appears at the last second to help
him and Baby Yoda get to his ship and off

00:00:40.260 --> 00:00:41.359
the planet.

00:00:41.359 --> 00:00:46.159
That might be done, but neither of them are
safe; and if his usual spaceport is razed

00:00:46.159 --> 00:00:50.600
and the Mandalorian enclave is scattered because
of his actions, the only thing to ask is what

00:00:50.600 --> 00:00:51.749
happens next.

00:00:51.749 --> 00:00:56.780
During Chapter 3, we hear The Client speak
with Dr. Pershing just before Mando bombs

00:00:56.780 --> 00:01:01.170
his way into the compound, but the Client
seems to have already disappeared by the time

00:01:01.170 --> 00:01:03.350
Mando makes it to Baby Yoda.

00:01:03.350 --> 00:01:06.030
The Client clearly anticipates an attack.

00:01:06.030 --> 00:01:12.289
"Finish your business quickly, for I can no
longer guarantee your safety."

00:01:12.289 --> 00:01:16.930
Is this because he knows the Mandalorian's
intentions are compromised, or that having

00:01:16.930 --> 00:01:19.820
the child at all destroys their safety?

00:01:19.820 --> 00:01:21.479
And what's up with Dr. Pershing?

00:01:21.479 --> 00:01:26.140
Sharp-eyed fans have noticed that the doctor
has a patch on his sleeve that bears a resemblance

00:01:26.140 --> 00:01:32.070
to those worn by denizens of the clone-manufacturing
world of Kamino the same people who cloned

00:01:32.070 --> 00:01:33.070
the Fetts.

00:01:33.070 --> 00:01:37.960
If this insignia rings true, Dr. Pershing
may just want to clone Baby Yoda.

00:01:37.960 --> 00:01:42.630
Kamino's scientists burned through thousands
of failed clones just trying to get Jango

00:01:42.630 --> 00:01:44.409
Fett's clone prototype right.

00:01:44.409 --> 00:01:48.859
We don't know exactly where The Client sits
in Empire hierarchy, but he looks more like

00:01:48.859 --> 00:01:54.350
a former senator than a military officer;
his outfit is strikingly similar to Palpatine's

00:01:54.350 --> 00:01:57.229
senatorial robes from the Star Wars prequels.

00:01:57.229 --> 00:01:59.460
So what's The Client's true desire here?

00:01:59.460 --> 00:02:04.579
Though small, a creature from Yoda's species
has importance because of its natural Force

00:02:04.579 --> 00:02:05.579
sensitivity.

00:02:05.579 --> 00:02:09.660
Perhaps the Empire is keen on returning to
the concept of a clone army... but populated

00:02:09.660 --> 00:02:12.000
by unstoppable Force users.

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:16.630
That would obviously be very effective in
overturning the power of the New Republic.

00:02:16.630 --> 00:02:21.570
The Client is interested in restoring, quote,
"the natural order of things after a period

00:02:21.570 --> 00:02:23.070
of disarray", after all.

00:02:23.070 --> 00:02:27.750
His sinister piece is not yet done, and we
will probably see him again.

00:02:27.750 --> 00:02:28.920
What does Mando want?

00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:33.810
It's been established that Mando is very suspicious
of the remnants of the Empire; he won't take

00:02:33.810 --> 00:02:37.790
Imperial Credits as payment, even if it means
being paid less for his work.

00:02:37.790 --> 00:02:41.280
"I don't know if you heard but the Empire
is gone."

00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:42.840
"It's all I've got!"

00:02:42.840 --> 00:02:47.990
Sure, he took the Baby Yoda job because he
wanted cash, but he was also in too deep to

00:02:47.990 --> 00:02:52.330
back out gracefully once he found out that
the Empire was the actual client.

00:02:52.330 --> 00:02:57.820
The Empire is responsible for what the Mandalorian
blacksmith ominously refers to as "the purge,"

00:02:57.820 --> 00:03:01.560
so that's a whole lot of incentive to not
like the Empire right away.

00:03:01.560 --> 00:03:06.090
So it makes sense that Mando would ask a lot
of questions about what's going to happen

00:03:06.090 --> 00:03:07.110
to Baby Yoda.

00:03:07.110 --> 00:03:11.450
But when speaking with the blacksmith, Mando
refers to the baby as his enemy, and goes

00:03:11.450 --> 00:03:16.070
on to say that the one who assisted him in
his fight with the mudhorn was unaware that

00:03:16.070 --> 00:03:18.950
they were enemies, which is a whole lot to
unpack.

00:03:18.950 --> 00:03:22.900
This all raises several questions: What's
Mando looking for?

00:03:22.900 --> 00:03:27.450
Is the baby still his enemy, and is he invested
in creating a relationship with it?

00:03:27.450 --> 00:03:31.980
Evidence suggests the latter, since he and
his Mandalorian friends shot up a whole town

00:03:31.980 --> 00:03:33.840
to save Baby Yoda's life.

00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:38.670
It's all a familial excursion, implying that
while they probably don't understand what

00:03:38.670 --> 00:03:43.390
Mando is doing, they'll support him and trust
he's acting in accord with their version of

00:03:43.390 --> 00:03:44.390
honor.

00:03:44.390 --> 00:03:49.070
One thing is for sure: a substantial proportion
of the scummiest citizens in the sector are

00:03:49.070 --> 00:03:51.650
going to be after him now because of all this.

